```csharp
Dictionary<string, string> dict = new();
var keys = dict.Keys;
dict.Add("a", "");
Console.WriteLine(keys.Count);
```
The code above returns `1` and this behavior is well-defined:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.generic.dictionary-2.keys?view=net-7.0#remarks
> The returned Dictionary<TKey,TValue>.KeyCollection is not a static copy; instead, the Dictionary<TKey,TValue>.KeyCollection refers back to the keys in the original Dictionary<TKey,TValue>. Therefore, changes to the Dictionary<TKey,TValue> continue to be reflected in the Dictionary<TKey,TValue>.KeyCollection.

However, `ConcurrentDictionary.Keys` returns a static copy: ([seems not documented](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.concurrentdictionary-2.keys?view=net-7.0))
```csharp
ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> cd = new();
var ckeys = cd.Keys;
cd.TryAdd("a", "");
Console.WriteLine(ckeys.Count); // print 0
```
I don't think it's a bug. And the behavior is unlikely to be changed. 



Anyway, we should fix `ReadOnlyDictionary.Keys` for no guarantee that the underlying `IDictionary`'s `Keys` returns a dynamic collection.
Similar situation on `Values`.

Hi @eiriktsarpalis 
I just ran into this problem that `ReadOnlyDictionary.Values` (from `ConcurrentDictionary`) only returns the values from the first access.

Is there a solution for this?

Would the following be a workaround for now?
 (I realize that using `ValueByKey = _valueByKey;` doesn't really make the dictionary `ReadOnly`, since you can just cast it back, but as a self-protection it should do for now)


Broken Code:

```cs
public class ContainerAsReadOnly
{
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<int, string> _valueByKey = [];
    public           ReadOnlyDictionary<int, string>   ValueByKey { get; }

    public ContainerAsReadOnly()
    {
        ValueByKey = _valueByKey.AsReadOnly(); // With this ValueByKey.Values is wrong
    }

    public void TryAdd(int key, string value) => _valueByKey.TryAdd(key, value);
}
```

Fix:
```cs
public class ContainerIReadOnlyCasting
{
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<int, string> _valueByKey = [];
    public           IReadOnlyDictionary<int, string>  ValueByKey { get; }
    public           IReadOnlyCollection<string>       Values     => (IReadOnlyCollection<string>)_valueByKey.Values;

    public ContainerIReadOnlyCasting()
    {
        ValueByKey = _valueByKey;
    }

    public void TryAdd(int key, string value) => _valueByKey.TryAdd(key, value);
}
```

