> Is that an issue though? 

Why wouldn't it be? You're changing from a cached snapshot to a new snapshot on every access.  From a performance perspective, every access to ReadOnlyDictionary.Keys will now be copying the full collection rather than being ammortized allocation-free.  And from a behavior perspective, it means that code like:
```C#
ICollection<TKey> keys = GetReadOnlyDictionary().Keys;
TKey[] array = new TKey[keys.Count];
keys.CopyTo(array, 0);
```
might now blow up in situations it didn't use to, e.g. because the collection might be growing concurrently, but the cached snapshot prevents concurrency-related problems.
 
We might say we think the new behavior is better.  But it's absolutely a breaking change and it should not be made non-chalantly.

Somewhat of an ad-hoc solution, but this could work as well without the concurrency issues:
```C#
private readonly bool _cacheCollections;

public ReadOnlyDictionary(IDictionary<TKey, TValue> dictionary)
{
    _cacheCollections = dictionary.GetType() != typeof(ConcurrentDictionary<TKey, TValue>);
}

public KeyCollection Keys => _cacheCollections ? _keys ??= new(m_dictionary.Keys) : new(m_dictionary.Keys);
```

> Yes, which is why I said if we're really considering making a breaking change like this, we should consider instead making it in CD. Either way, it's a non-trivial impact.

I would expect that a change on the CD collections directly would have substantially more impact, since the example you provided works fine when applied to CD directly:
```C#
ICollection<TKey> keys = concurrentDictionary.Keys;
TKey[] array = new TKey[keys.Count];
keys.CopyTo(array, 0);
```
But is fundamentally broken when applied to a ROD wrapper per #93829, making it less likely for user code to depend on it.
